"""Scene definitions for the Incident Response: Prepare, Contain, Eradicate, Recover, Learn teaching video (generated from concept.toml)."""

SCENES = [
    dict(key='01-poster', kind='poster', title='Incident Response: Prepare, Contain, Eradicate, Recover, Learn',
         body=None,
         narration='Hello, and welcome. [[slnc 400]] This video explains incident response: prepare, contain, eradicate, recover, and learn. [[slnc 300]] This video is presented by Jayasekhar Konduru. [[slnc 600]] First, a simple definition. [[slnc 300]] Incident response is what an organisation does once it is breached. [[slnc 300]] The outcome is mostly decided before it starts, by what was prepared, and then by the order of the first hour: contain without destroying evidence, find the full scope, close every way back in, rebuild, and watch. [[slnc 600]] Think of a fire drill. [[slnc 300]] The building that practised gets everyone out, and knows which doors to close. [[slnc 600]] Our example is a breach at an online shop.'),
    dict(key='02-scenario', kind='bullets', title='The Scenario',
         body=['An online shop: a web server and a database.', '', 'An attacker gets in through a flaw,', 'copies 50,000 customers,', 'and leaves three ways back in.', '', 'The same breach is replayed three times,', 'with different preparation and responses.'],
         narration='Here is the scenario. [[slnc 400]] An online shop has a web server and a database. [[slnc 300]] At one in the morning, an attacker gets in through a flaw, copies fifty thousand customers, and leaves three ways back in. [[slnc 500]] The demo replays that same breach three times. [[slnc 300]] Once with nothing prepared, once with only an alert, and once with a full playbook. [[slnc 500]] Then it compares how each one ends.'),
    dict(key='03-words', kind='bullets', title='The Words',
         body=['Incident: a breach, or a likely one', 'Way back in: what lets the attacker return', 'Contain: stop the damage, keep the evidence', 'Scope: what was touched, and since when', 'Eradicate: close every way back in', 'Runbook, playbook: the written plan'],
         narration='Before the demos, the words. [[slnc 500]] An incident is a breach, or something that is probably one. [[slnc 500]] A way back in, also called a foothold or persistence, is anything that lets the attacker return: a scheduled job, a key, an account, a stolen password. [[slnc 500]] To contain is to stop the damage, without destroying the evidence. [[slnc 500]] The scope is what the attacker touched, and since when. [[slnc 500]] To eradicate is to close every way back in. [[slnc 500]] And a runbook, or playbook, is the written plan, with names and steps, made before it is needed.'),
    dict(key='04-letters-1', kind='bullets', title='What the Letters Stand For',
         body=['SSH: Secure Shell: remote login to a server', 'NIST: National Institute of Standards and Technology (United States)', 'SP 800-61: NIST Special Publication 800-61: the incident handling guide', 'cron: the Unix scheduler that runs a job at set times', "db01, web01: the shop's database server and web server"],
         narration="A few letters come up again and again, so here is what they stand for. [[slnc 400]] S S H is secure shell, the usual way to log in to a server remotely. An S S H key lets someone log in without a password. [[slnc 400]] N I S T is the national institute of standards and technology, in the United States. [[slnc 400]] S P eight hundred sixty one is N I S T special publication eight hundred sixty one, the guide to handling security incidents. [[slnc 400]] cron is the Unix scheduler. A cron job runs a command again and again, at set times. [[slnc 400]] web zero one is the shop's web server, and d b zero one is its database server."),
    dict(key='05-ways-back-in', kind='figure', title='Five ways back in, and what closes each',
         body='ways_back_in',
         narration="Let's see the core idea on one example: the ways back in. [[slnc 400]] By twenty to two, the attacker can come back in five ways. [[slnc 600]] On the left, the ways back in. [[slnc 300]] A cron job and an S S H key on the web server. [[slnc 300]] A hidden admin account called svc backup. [[slnc 300]] The flaw they came in through. [[slnc 300]] And the database password they stole. [[slnc 700]] On the right, what closes each one. [[slnc 300]] A rebuild from a clean image removes the cron job and the key together. [[slnc 300]] The hidden account must first be found in the logs, and then disabled. [[slnc 300]] The flaw needs a patch, and the password a rotation. [[slnc 600]] Five ways, four different actions. [[slnc 300]] Leave one open, and the attacker comes back."),
    dict(key='06-act-1-figure', kind='figure', title="The attacker's script, and the rows it copies",
         body='attack_timeline',
         narration="First demo: the attack. [[slnc 400]] At the top, the input: the attacker's script, the same in all three runs. [[slnc 300]] At one o'clock, they get into the web server, web zero one, through a flaw in the application. [[slnc 300]] At five past one, they read the database password from its configuration. [[slnc 300]] At twenty past one, they start copying the customer table. [[slnc 300]] And at twenty to two, they add three ways back in. [[slnc 700]] At the bottom, the result: the rows copied. [[slnc 300]] One thousand rows a minute, from twenty past one. [[slnc 300]] Fifty thousand customers, divided by one thousand a minute, is fifty minutes, so at ten past two every row is gone. [[slnc 300]] At half past one, ten thousand rows are gone. [[slnc 300]] At twenty five to two, fifteen thousand. [[slnc 600]] Every minute that nobody acts costs another thousand customers."),
    dict(key='07-act-1-1', kind='console', title='Act One — The attack',
         body="ONE. The attack.\n  THE ATTACKER'S SCRIPT (the same in all three runs):\n    01:00  gets into web01 through a flaw in the application\n    01:05  reads the database password from the configuration\n    01:20  starts copying the customer table from db01\n    01:40  adds three ways back in: cron job, SSH key, svc-backup\n  HOW FAST THE DATA GOES:\n    rate      1000 rows a minute, from 01:20\n    table     50000 customers\n    all gone  50000 / 1000 = 50 minutes later, at 02:10",
         narration="Here is the real output. [[slnc 400]] The attacker's script, in four steps. [[slnc 300]] Then how fast the data goes: one thousand rows a minute, fifty thousand customers, so fifty thousand divided by one thousand is fifty minutes, and all is gone at ten past two."),
    dict(key='08-act-1-2', kind='console', title='Act One — The attack',
         body='ONE. (continued)\n  THE WAYS BACK IN, AND WHAT CLOSES EACH:\n    cron job on web01                  rebuild from the image\n    SSH key on web01                   rebuild from the image\n    hidden admin account svc-backup    find in logs, then disable\n    unpatched flaw in the application  patch the flaw\n    stolen database password           rotate the password',
         narration='Then the five ways back in, and what closes each one. [[slnc 300]] The cron job and the S S H key live on web zero one, so a rebuild from a clean image removes both. [[slnc 300]] The hidden admin account must first be found in the logs, then disabled. [[slnc 300]] The flaw needs a patch, and the stolen password needs rotating.'),
    dict(key='09-act-2-figure', kind='figure', title='What was prepared, and when it is found',
         body='checklist',
         narration='Second demo: what was prepared. [[slnc 400]] At the top, the input: six things an organisation decides before any attack. [[slnc 300]] A runbook with a contact list. [[slnc 200]] An alert on unusual database reads. [[slnc 200]] Logs kept centrally for at least seven days. [[slnc 200]] A way to snapshot memory and disk. [[slnc 200]] A known good image to rebuild from. [[slnc 200]] And tested backups. [[slnc 600]] Three organisations. [[slnc 300]] The first has none of them: zero of six. [[slnc 300]] The second has only the alert: one of six. [[slnc 300]] The third has all six. [[slnc 700]] At the bottom, the result: when the breach is found. [[slnc 300]] Without the alert, a customer finds it, three days later. [[slnc 300]] Three times twenty four times sixty is four thousand three hundred and twenty minutes. [[slnc 300]] With the alert, it is found thirty minutes in, at half past one.'),
    dict(key='10-act-2', kind='console', title='Act Two — What was prepared',
         body='TWO. What was prepared.\n  THE SIX THINGS, DECIDED BEFORE THE ATTACK:\n                                     nothing   alert    full\n    runbook and contact list              no      no     yes\n    alert on unusual database reads       no     yes     yes\n    logs kept centrally 7+ days           no      no     yes\n    snapshot of memory and disk           no      no     yes\n    known-good image to rebuild           no      no     yes\n    tested backups                        no      no     yes\n    in place                          0 of 6  1 of 6  6 of 6\n  WHEN THE BREACH IS FOUND:\n    nothing     by a customer  3 x 24 x 60 = 4320 min, day 3 01:00\n    alert only  by the alert   30 min, day 0 01:30\n    full        by the alert   30 min, day 0 01:30',
         narration='Here is the real output. [[slnc 400]] The checklist: a column per organisation, yes or no for each thing, and the count in place. [[slnc 400]] Then when each one finds the breach: a customer on day three, or the alert at half past one.'),
    dict(key='11-act-3-figure', kind='figure', title='Response A: before, the five steps, and after',
         body='response_a',
         narration="Third demo: response A, with nothing prepared. [[slnc 400]] On the left, the state before the response. [[slnc 300]] It is day three at one o'clock, when a customer complains. [[slnc 300]] All fifty thousand rows have been gone since ten past two on the first night, and all five ways back in are open. [[slnc 700]] In the middle, the response: five steps. [[slnc 300]] They reboot web zero one, which wipes the memory evidence. [[slnc 300]] They rotate the database password, and reconnect. [[slnc 300]] Two hours later, the attacker is back. [[slnc 700]] On the right, the result. [[slnc 300]] Only the password is closed; four ways back in stay open. [[slnc 300]] No evidence, no root cause, and the attacker came back through the cron job, which a reboot does not remove."),
    dict(key='12-act-3-1', kind='console', title='Act Three — Response A: nothing prepared, and a panicked fix',
         body='THREE. Response A: nothing prepared, and a panicked fix.\n  BEFORE THE RESPONSE (day 3 01:00):\n    rows copied   50000 of 50000, all gone since day 0 02:10\n    ways back in  5 of 5 open\n  THE RESPONSE (day 3):\n    01:00  detect              a customer says their data is sold\n    01:03  reboot web01        memory lost; cron job, SSH key stay\n    01:13  rotate db password  the stolen password stops working\n    01:23  reconnect web01     back in service, 4 ways in open\n    03:23  monitor 2 hours     THE ATTACKER IS BACK',
         narration='Here is the real output. [[slnc 400]] Before the response: all fifty thousand rows copied, and five of five ways open. [[slnc 400]] Then the response, step by step, with the time and the effect of each step. [[slnc 300]] The last line: the attacker is back.'),
    dict(key='13-act-3-2', kind='console', title='Act Three — Response A: nothing prepared, and a panicked fix',
         body='THREE. (continued)\n  AFTER THE RESPONSE:\n    copying stopped  never: all 50000 rows were gone\n    ways back in     4 of 5 open\n      cron job on web01\n      SSH key on web01\n      hidden admin account svc-backup\n      unpatched flaw in the application\n    evidence         memory no, disk no, logs no\n    root cause       unknown: no central logs\n    out of service   01:03 to 01:23 = 20 min\n    the attacker     came back: cron job on web01',
         narration='After the response. [[slnc 300]] The copying was never stopped by the response: it had finished three days earlier. [[slnc 300]] Four of five ways back in are still open. [[slnc 300]] No memory, disk or logs were kept, and the root cause is unknown. [[slnc 300]] Out of service for twenty minutes. [[slnc 300]] And the attacker came back through the cron job.'),
    dict(key='14-act-4-figure', kind='figure', title='Response B: before, the five steps, and after',
         body='response_b',
         narration='Fourth demo: response B, an alert but no plan. [[slnc 400]] On the left, before. [[slnc 300]] The alert comes at half past one. [[slnc 300]] Half past one minus twenty past one is ten minutes, times one thousand rows, is ten thousand rows. [[slnc 700]] In the middle, the response. [[slnc 300]] At twenty five to two they isolate web zero one: a network rule cuts it off, its memory is kept, and the route to the database is cut. [[slnc 300]] Then, with no image, they rebuild it by hand, which takes two hours. [[slnc 300]] They reconnect at a quarter to four, and two hours later the attacker is back. [[slnc 700]] On the right, the result. [[slnc 300]] The rebuild removed the cron job and the S S H key. [[slnc 300]] But nobody knew about the hidden account, the flaw was not patched, and the password was not rotated. [[slnc 300]] The attacker came back through svc backup.'),
    dict(key='15-act-4-1', kind='console', title='Act Four — Response B: an alert, but no plan',
         body='FOUR. Response B: an alert, but no plan.\n  BEFORE THE RESPONSE (day 0 01:30):\n    rows copied   (01:30 - 01:20) x 1000 = 10000 of 50000\n    ways back in  5 of 5 open\n  THE RESPONSE (day 0):\n    01:30  detect              alert: unusual database reads\n    01:35  isolate web01       memory kept, route to db01 cut\n    03:35  rebuild web01       by hand, no image: is it clean?\n    03:45  reconnect web01     back in service, 3 ways in open\n    05:45  monitor 2 hours     THE ATTACKER IS BACK',
         narration='Here is the real output. [[slnc 400]] Before: ten thousand of fifty thousand rows copied, and five ways open. [[slnc 400]] The response: detect, isolate, rebuild by hand, reconnect, and monitor. [[slnc 300]] The attacker is back.'),
    dict(key='16-act-4-2', kind='console', title='Act Four — Response B: an alert, but no plan',
         body='FOUR. (continued)\n  AFTER THE RESPONSE:\n    copying stopped  (01:35 - 01:20) x 1000 = 15000 rows\n    ways back in     3 of 5 open\n      hidden admin account svc-backup\n      unpatched flaw in the application\n      stolen database password\n    evidence         memory no, disk no, logs no\n    root cause       unknown: no central logs\n    out of service   01:35 to 03:45 = 130 min\n    the attacker     came back: hidden admin account svc-backup',
         narration='After the response. [[slnc 300]] The copying stopped at twenty five to two: fifteen minutes times one thousand is fifteen thousand rows. [[slnc 300]] Three ways back in are open. [[slnc 300]] No snapshot was taken before the rebuild, so there is no evidence. [[slnc 300]] Out of service for one hundred and thirty minutes, and the attacker came back through the hidden admin account.'),
    dict(key='17-act-5-figure', kind='figure', title='Response C: before, the eleven steps, and after',
         body='response_c',
         narration="Fifth demo: response C, the playbook. [[slnc 400]] On the left, before: the same alert at half past one, the same ten thousand rows, and five ways open. [[slnc 700]] In the middle, eleven steps. [[slnc 300]] Contain first: isolate web zero one, and end the attacker's database session. [[slnc 300]] Then capture memory, disk and logs, with hashes, before anything is changed. [[slnc 300]] Then scope from the central logs: the attack began at one o'clock, and svc backup was made at twenty to two. [[slnc 300]] Then close every way in: rotate the password, disable svc backup, patch the flaw, and rebuild from the image. [[slnc 300]] Reconnect, and watch. [[slnc 700]] On the right, the result. [[slnc 300]] All five ways back in are closed. [[slnc 300]] Fifteen thousand rows, the same as B, the evidence kept, and two quiet hours."),
    dict(key='18-act-5-1', kind='console', title='Act Five — Response C: the playbook',
         body="FIVE. Response C: the playbook.\n  BEFORE THE RESPONSE (day 0 01:30):\n    rows copied   (01:30 - 01:20) x 1000 = 10000 of 50000\n    ways back in  5 of 5 open\n  THE RESPONSE (day 0):\n    01:30  detect              alert: unusual database reads\n    01:35  isolate web01       memory kept, route to db01 cut\n    01:37  kill db sessions    attacker's db01 session ended\n    01:52  capture evidence    memory, disk and logs, with hashes\n    02:12  scope from logs     start 01:00; svc-backup made 01:40\n    02:22  rotate db password  the stolen password stops working",
         narration='Here is the real output. [[slnc 400]] Before: the same ten thousand rows, and five open ways. [[slnc 400]] The first six steps: detect, isolate, kill the database sessions, capture the evidence, scope from the logs, and rotate the password.'),
    dict(key='19-act-5-2', kind='console', title='Act Five — Response C: the playbook',
         body='FIVE. (continued)\n    02:25  disable svc-backup  hidden admin account removed\n    02:55  patch the flaw      the way in is closed\n    03:35  rebuild web01       from the image: cron job, key gone\n    03:45  reconnect web01     back in service, 0 ways in open\n    05:45  monitor 2 hours     quiet: nobody came back\n  AFTER THE RESPONSE:\n    copying stopped  (01:35 - 01:20) x 1000 = 15000 rows\n    ways back in     0 of 5 open\n    evidence         memory yes, disk yes, logs yes\n    root cause       known: 30 days of central logs reach 01:00\n    out of service   01:35 to 03:45 = 130 min\n    the attacker     did not come back',
         narration="The last five steps: disable svc backup, patch the flaw, rebuild from the image, reconnect, and two quiet hours. [[slnc 400]] After the response: fifteen thousand rows, zero of five ways open, and memory, disk and logs kept. [[slnc 300]] The root cause is known, because thirty days of central logs reach back to one o'clock. [[slnc 300]] And the attacker did not come back."),
    dict(key='20-act-6-figure', kind='figure', title='The review: the same breach, three responses',
         body='review',
         narration='Sixth demo: the review. [[slnc 400]] The input is the three runs; the result is this table. [[slnc 300]] Green is good, rose is bad. [[slnc 600]] Response A: found after three days, the copying never stopped, four ways open, no evidence, and the attacker came back. [[slnc 300]] Its outage was the shortest, twenty minutes, and it still did not recover. [[slnc 600]] Response B: found in thirty minutes, fifteen thousand rows, but three ways open, and the attacker came back. [[slnc 600]] Response C: the same fifteen thousand rows and the same outage as B, but no way open, the evidence kept, the cause known, and recovered. [[slnc 600]] At the bottom: B and C did the same first two steps. [[slnc 300]] At step three, B rebuilt by hand, while C ended the database session, and then took evidence and scoped from the logs. [[slnc 300]] That is the decision a blameless review looks for.'),
    dict(key='21-act-6-1', kind='console', title='Act Six — The review',
         body='SIX. The review.\n  THE THREE RESPONSES SIDE BY SIDE:\n                                       A       B       C\n    found after                   3 days  30 min  30 min\n    rows copied before the stop    never   15000   15000\n    ways back in left open             4       3       0\n    evidence kept                     no      no     yes\n    root cause known                  no      no     yes\n    web01 out of service (min)        20     130     130\n    the attacker came back           yes     yes      no\n    recovered                         no      no     yes',
         narration='Here is the real output: the three responses side by side, one row for each question of the review. [[slnc 400]] Only C recovered.'),
    dict(key='22-act-6-2', kind='console', title='Act Six — The review',
         body='SIX. (continued)\n  WHERE B AND C PART WAYS:\n    the same      detect, isolate web01\n    B, step 3     03:35  rebuild web01\n    C, step 3     01:37  kill db sessions',
         narration='And where B and C part ways: the same detect and isolate. [[slnc 300]] Then at step three, B rebuilds by hand, finished at three thirty five, while C kills the database sessions at one thirty seven.'),
    dict(key='23-costs', kind='bullets', title='What It Costs',
         body=['Preparation costs money before it pays', 'Containment hurts the business', 'Evidence and speed pull against each other', 'Rebuilding is slower than cleaning', 'Logs kept for weeks cost storage'],
         narration='Nothing is free. [[slnc 400]] Here is what it costs. [[slnc 500]] Preparation costs money long before any benefit is visible. [[slnc 500]] Containment hurts the business: the shop is offline while the server is cut off. [[slnc 500]] Capturing evidence takes time, while everyone wants speed. [[slnc 500]] Rebuilding from a clean image is slower than cleaning by hand, but only the rebuild is sure. [[slnc 300]] And keeping logs for weeks costs storage.'),
    dict(key='24-recognise', kind='bullets', title='Where You Have Seen It',
         body=['Runbooks and on-call rotations', '"Isolate, do not power off" in forensic guidance', 'A breach found by a customer, days later', 'Blameless post-incident reviews'],
         narration='You have probably met this already. [[slnc 400]] Runbooks and on-call rotations are preparation. [[slnc 500]] Isolate, do not power off, in forensic guidance, is containment that keeps the evidence in memory. [[slnc 500]] A breach found by a customer, days later, is what happens with no alert. [[slnc 500]] And a blameless post-incident review is the learn phase, done well. [[slnc 500]] The N I S T guide, special publication eight hundred sixty one, names these same phases.'),
    dict(key='25-verdict', kind='bullets', title='How To Use It',
         body=['Prepare: alert, central logs, snapshots,', '  a known-good image, a runbook', 'Contain by isolating, not rebooting', 'Scope the incident from the logs', 'Close every way back in; rebuild from the image', 'Recover, and watch; then review'],
         narration='So, how do you use it? [[slnc 400]] Prepare before anything happens: an alert, central logs, a way to take snapshots, a known-good image, and a runbook. [[slnc 500]] Contain by isolating the server, not by rebooting it. [[slnc 300]] Scope the incident from the logs. [[slnc 500]] Close every way back in: rotate the stolen secrets, remove the hidden accounts, patch the flaw, and rebuild from the image. [[slnc 500]] Then reconnect, watch closely, and review what happened.'),
    dict(key='26-classes', kind='bullets', title='The Java Classes',
         body=['Preparation: the six things decided before the attack', "World: the shop's systems, the attacker and the clock", 'Responder: the actions, each with a duration and an effect', 'Playbooks: the three responses, as orders of actions', 'Outcome: the review of one run'],
         narration='Now, a quick look at the code. [[slnc 300]] The shop, the attacker and the clock are a small simulation, so every run gives the same timeline. [[slnc 300]] Here are the classes that matter.'),
    dict(key='27-class-1', kind='code', title='Preparation — the checklist',
         body='// Preparation: the six things decided before the attack\npublic Map<String, Boolean> checklist() {\n    ...\n    c.put("alert on unusual database reads", monitoringOnDatabaseReads);\n    c.put("logs kept centrally 7+ days",\n            logsShippedCentrally && logRetentionDays >= 7);',
         narration='Preparation is a record of six choices. [[slnc 300]] The checklist names each one and says whether it is in place. [[slnc 300]] Logs only count when they are central and kept for at least seven days.'),
    dict(key='28-class-2', kind='code', title='World — the copying goes on until it is cut',
         body="// World: the shop's systems, the attacker and the clock\nboolean exfilActive() {\n    return minute >= EXFIL_START && exfiltrated < CUSTOMERS\n            && dbSessionAlive && !webIsolated;",
         narration='Each simulated minute, the attacker copies another thousand rows while this is true. [[slnc 300]] Isolating web zero one, or ending the database session, makes it false.'),
    dict(key='29-class-3', kind='code', title='World — the ways back in that are left',
         body='// World: the shop\'s systems, the attacker and the clock\nif (cron && !webRebuilt) {\n    left.add("cron job on web01");\n...\nif (adminBackdoor) {\n    left.add("hidden admin account svc-backup");',
         narration='The ways back in are plain flags. [[slnc 300]] A rebuild clears the cron job and the key; only disabling the account clears the account.'),
    dict(key='30-class-4', kind='code', title='Responder — isolate: memory kept, route cut',
         body='// Responder: the actions, each with a duration and an effect\npublic Responder isolateWeb01() {\n    w.advance(5);\n    w.webIsolated = true;',
         narration="Each action moves the clock forward, and the attacker keeps working during those minutes. [[slnc 300]] Isolation stops the copying and keeps web zero one's memory."),
    dict(key='31-class-5', kind='code', title='Responder — reboot: evidence lost, persistence kept',
         body='// Responder: the actions, each with a duration and an effect\nboolean lost = !w.memoryCaptured;\nw.memoryCaptured = false;\nlog("reboot web01",\n        (lost ? "memory lost; " : "") + "cron job, SSH key stay");',
         narration='A reboot wipes the memory, and changes nothing else. [[slnc 300]] The cron job and the key are on the disk, so they survive.'),
    dict(key='32-class-6', kind='code', title='Responder — you cannot disable what you do not know',
         body='// Responder: the actions, each with a duration and an effect\nif (w.scoped) {\n    w.adminBackdoor = false;',
         narration='The hidden account is only disabled when the logs were scoped first. [[slnc 300]] That is why response B leaves it open.'),
    dict(key='33-class-7', kind='code', title="Playbooks — the playbook's order",
         body='// Playbooks: the three responses, as orders of actions\nnew Responder(w).detect()\n        .isolateWeb01().killDatabaseSessions() // contain\n        .captureEvidence().scopeFromLogs() // analyse\n        .rotateDatabasePassword().disableBackdoorAccount()\n        .patchVulnerability().rebuildWeb01() // eradicate\n        .reconnect().monitorTwoHours(); // recover',
         narration='A response is just an order of actions. [[slnc 300]] The playbook contains, then analyses, then eradicates, then recovers. [[slnc 300]] Change the order, and the outcome changes.'),
    dict(key='34-class-8', kind='code', title='Outcome — recovered means nothing left open',
         body='// Outcome: the review of one run\npublic boolean recovered() {\n    return footholdsLeft.isEmpty() && !attackerReturned;',
         narration='A run counts as recovered only when no way back in is left and the attacker did not return. [[slnc 300]] A short outage alone is not recovery.'),
    dict(key='35-outro', kind='outro', title='Thanks for Watching',
         body=['Full source, notes, diagrams and an animated walkthrough', 'are in the repository. Add a rate limit on the database', 'account and see how many rows it saves.'],
         narration="That's incident response. [[slnc 400]] If you remember one sentence, make it this one. [[slnc 300]] The outcome of an incident is mostly decided before it starts, and by the order of the first hour: contain without destroying evidence, scope from logs, close every way back in, rebuild, and watch. [[slnc 500]] The full source code, written notes, diagrams, and an animated walkthrough are all in the repository. [[slnc 300]] It runs on your own machine. [[slnc 500]] Here is one exercise to try. [[slnc 300]] Add a rate limit on the database account to the preparation, and see how many rows it saves. [[slnc 500]] If you found this useful, a like really helps other people discover it. [[slnc 300]] Kindly subscribe to the channel. [[slnc 300]] It helps the channel grow and bring you more content. [[slnc 400]] Thanks for watching!"),
]

